From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: QM1 Debian Maintainers <qm1-debian@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 5 Nov 2026 12:40:00 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 1/1] rtw88: extend the TX report purge timeout for the
 RTL8723BS

The vendor rtw88 tree arms the TX report purge timer with
RTW_TX_PROBE_TIMEOUT, which is 500 ms for every chip and every bus.  The
RTL8723BS reports late and unreliably, so the timer expires while frames
are still in flight: rtw_tx_report_purge_timer() then purges the whole
tx_report queue and drops those skbs without ever reporting them as
transmitted.  The kernel retries them, TCP retransmits, and the link
looks healthy while the throughput collapses -- exactly the "network
stops flowing" symptom this device is known for.

Upstream already raised that timeout for this chip: commit 4d688f22ebbf
("wifi: rtw88: tx: extend the TX report purge timeout to RTL8723BS",
merged in the Linux 7.4 merge window) gives the 8723BS 2500 ms, and
current upstream master generalises it to RTW_TX_PROBE_TIMEOUT_LONG
(2500 ms) for every bus except PCIe.  The vendor tree this project
builds from never picked that up, so apply the same value here.

Without this patch the image would keep the 500 ms timeout and could
still show the very symptom the rtw88 backport was supposed to cure.
---
 tx.c | 21 ++++++++++++++++++++++----
 1 file changed, 19 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

--- a/tx.c
+++ b/tx.c
@@ -235,6 +235,7 @@
 void rtw_tx_report_enqueue(struct rtw_dev *rtwdev, struct sk_buff *skb, u8 sn)
 {
 	struct rtw_tx_report *tx_report = &rtwdev->tx_report;
+	unsigned long timeout;
 	unsigned long flags;
 	u8 *drv_data;
 
@@ -246,7 +247,25 @@
 	__skb_queue_tail(&tx_report->queue, skb);
 	spin_unlock_irqrestore(&tx_report->q_lock, flags);
 
-	mod_timer(&tx_report->purge_timer, jiffies + RTW_TX_PROBE_TIMEOUT);
+	/*
+	 * The RTL8723BS reports late and unreliably.  With the default 500 ms
+	 * purge timeout the timer expires while frames are still in flight and
+	 * the whole queue is dropped, so the kernel never learns that those
+	 * frames were transmitted and TCP stalls -- the classic "the link is
+	 * up but the throughput collapses" symptom.
+	 *
+	 * Upstream extended the same timeout for this chip (rtw88: extend the
+	 * TX report purge timeout to RTL8723BS, commit 4d688f22ebbf, merged in
+	 * the Linux 7.4 merge window) and later generalised it to 2500 ms for
+	 * every bus except PCIe.  Adopt the same value here.
+	 */
+	if (rtwdev->chip->id == RTW_CHIP_TYPE_8723B &&
+	    rtw_hci_type(rtwdev) == RTW_HCI_TYPE_SDIO)
+		timeout = msecs_to_jiffies(2500);
+	else
+		timeout = RTW_TX_PROBE_TIMEOUT;
+
+	mod_timer(&tx_report->purge_timer, jiffies + timeout);
 }
 EXPORT_SYMBOL(rtw_tx_report_enqueue);
 
